      "dom_id": "question-313149",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2}=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} & =\\frac{(-2)^{2}-5(-2)-14}{-2+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4+10-14}{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the limit, we have to factor the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-5x-14}{x+2} & =\\frac{(x-7)(x+2)}{x+2} \\\\  & =x-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}(x-7) & =(-2)-7 \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
